@@s029 Outputs obtained by running the file with Scala 3.8.4 and ScalaCheck 1.19. When you hand in, leave <code>import lazyList00.*</code> active: the grader changes that line itself. Never write <code>lazyList00.something</code> in the code: everything must go through the import at the top.
@@s030 The same tests, three implementations
@@s031 The exercise file, line by line
@@s032 The file is in the package <code>adpro.lazyList</code>, so <code>import lazyList00.*</code> means <code>adpro.lazyList.lazyList00.*</code>: the folder <code>subjects/lazyList00</code>. From here comes the type <code>LazyList</code> that the tests see. To try another implementation one puts <code>//</code> in front of this line and removes it from another. Only one active at a time.
@@s033 Brings in the functions of the companion object of the imported LazyList: <code>empty</code>, <code>cons</code>, <code>from</code>, … So one writes <code>cons(1, empty)</code> instead of <code>LazyList.cons(1, LazyList.empty)</code>.
@@s034 The container of the tests. <code>"testing"</code> is the prefix that appears in the output: <code>+ testing.Ex01.01: …</code>.
@@s035 Converts a List into a LazyList. <code>la*</code> "unrolls" the list into the arguments of <code>LazyList.apply(as: A*)</code>: <code>LazyList(List(1, 2)*)</code> = <code>LazyList(1, 2)</code>.
@@s036 Generator of non-empty LazyLists. <code>using Arbitrary[A]</code>: to make lists of A one must be able to generate As (for Int there already is one). It generates a random List, keeps only the non-empty ones (<code>suchThat</code>), and converts it.
@@s037 Ex01.01, scenario test: a Boolean. The <code>headOption</code> of the empty list must be None.
@@s038 Ex01.02. The <code>given</code> holds only inside this test: the <code>LazyList[Int]</code> of forAll will be non-empty. Two pieces joined with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>: a list of a single element n must give Some(n) (label <code>singleton</code>); a random non-empty list must not give None (label <code>random</code>).
@@s039 Doubt: what differences are there between the 3 lists and the 3 checks?
@@s040 The three folders are the <b>same file</b>, except for one piece. <code>lazyList01</code> changes only <code>headOption</code>: it adds <code>case Empty =&gt; ???</code> in front of the right version. <code>lazyList02</code> changes only the last line of <code>cons</code>: <code>Empty</code> in place of <code>Cons(() =&gt; head, () =&gt; tail)</code>. The checks are three, because Ex01.02 has two pieces (<code>singleton</code> and <code>random</code>). Separating them and running them one by one:
@@s041 Ex01.01<br><code>empty.headOption == None</code>
@@s042 singleton<br><code>cons(n, empty).headOption == Some(n)</code>
@@s043 random<br><code>s.headOption != None</code>
@@s044 <code>lazyList00</code>
@@s045 None == None ✓
@@s046 Some(n) == Some(n) ✓
@@s047 Some(…) != None ✓
@@s048 <code>lazyList01</code>
@@s049 <b>??? → NotImplementedError</b>
@@s050 ✓ (never goes through Empty)
@@s051 <code>lazyList02</code>
@@s052 ✓ (empty is empty anyway)
@@s053 <b>None ≠ Some(0)</b>: Falsified
@@s054 <b>Falsified, ARG_0: Empty</b>
@@s055 With <code>lazyList02</code> <b>two</b> pieces fail, but the output of Ex01.02 says only <code>singleton</code>: with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> ScalaCheck reports the label of the first piece that fails. The <code>random</code> piece fails with <code>ARG_0: Empty</code>: the "non-empty" list is empty, because <code>list2lazyList</code> too uses the broken <code>cons</code> (checked).
